[
    {
        "content": "<p>A friend of mine had a very interesting <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mwe.html\">#mwe</a></p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Logic</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Function</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Basic</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"o\">):</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">Function</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">update</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">DecidableEq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"o\">]:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">Function</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">update</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>See the inferred type of <code>P</code> in each case.</p>\n<p>It seems if I don't assign a DecidableEq typeclass assumption for P, lean concludes that P must be a <code>Prop</code> instead of throwing a missing instance error.</p>",
        "id": 625740993,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1789997925
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>On the one hand it makes sense that lean saw it needed to infer a DecidableEq instance for P in <code>Function.update</code>. On the other hand, this seems backwards to assume a Sort is Prop because of some typeclass inference weirdness. Unless my diagnosis is wrong and something totally different is happening. </p>\n<p>Either way, it seems silly and backward that <code>P</code> should be assumed to be <code>Prop</code> instead of getting a missing instance error in the first example.</p>",
        "id": 625741859,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1789998141
    },
    {
        "content": "<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Logic</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Function</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Basic</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">synth</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">DecidableEq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- succeeds, P is Prop</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">synth</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Sort</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">DecidableEq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- succeeds, P is Prop</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>is more minimal</p>",
        "id": 625743437,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1789998493
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yeah but the example above shows how weird it is. If I had to guess, typeclass inference happens before <del>implicit</del> variables got assigned a type. The expected behaviour would be that <code>P</code> was a universe polymorphic Type, and lean complained that it failed to synthesize a <code>DecidableEq</code> instance for <code>P</code> as seems to happen whenever I miss such an instance.</p>",
        "id": 625744429,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1789998708
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Note that this is not a problem with implicits or autoImplicits. It also happens when I make <code>P</code> an explicit parameter but without type annotations</p>",
        "id": 625747713,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1789999397
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think this can be summarized as \"typeclass synthesis (unexpectedly) assigns universe metavariables\"</p>",
        "id": 625748092,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1789999475
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I suspect this is needed by category theory, and amounts to the fact that there is no <code>outParam</code> mechanism for universes</p>",
        "id": 625748191,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1789999498
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Do they need it to work like this for <code>Prop</code>? I thought category theorists inhabit higher universes.</p>",
        "id": 625754840,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1790000899
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Isn't it backwards though that the body of the example is able to cause a unification of something which is a (level)metavariable since the type?</p>",
        "id": 625787192,
        "sender_full_name": "Edward van de Meent",
        "timestamp": 1790006402
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>No, <code>example</code> is <code>def</code>. Also, the body is irrelevant anyway here.</p>",
        "id": 625787312,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1790006420
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Same difference</p>",
        "id": 625787446,
        "sender_full_name": "Edward van de Meent",
        "timestamp": 1790006448
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Unification is happening due to TC inference in the statement, and not in any way due to the body</p>",
        "id": 625787619,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1790006482
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ah right, I was confused...</p>",
        "id": 625787923,
        "sender_full_name": "Edward van de Meent",
        "timestamp": 1790006549
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"310045\">Eric Wieser</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113488-general/topic/Weird.20defeq.20with.20and.20without.20DecidableEq.20instance/near/625748191\">schrieb</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>there is no <code>outParam</code> mechanism for universes</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I thought there was the <code>univ_out_params</code> attribute but I don't know the extent of it; perhaps it simply doesn't forbid assigning most universe metavariables.</p>",
        "id": 625863180,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1790027016
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>My friend constructed an even more innocuous looking example </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Logic</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Function</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Basic</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">structure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SimpleGraph</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">adj</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">isColoringOf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">col</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Fin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SimpleGraph</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">DecidableEq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">]:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">((</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">adj</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">col</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">col</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">constant_coloring_is_coloring</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SimpleGraph</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">isColoringOf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Fin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">unfold</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">isColoringOf</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨</span><span class=\"n\">h_adj</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"n\">h_neq</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">if</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">then</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">contradiction</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">else</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">contradiction</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>not a big surprise if you know lean. But definitely a “gotcha” for beginnners.</p>",
        "id": 625981970,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1790071846
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>From my POV not using variables that are not properly introduced before is one of the first thing one should learn.</p>",
        "id": 625982744,
        "sender_full_name": "Etienne Marion",
        "timestamp": 1790072080
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I comfortably use autoImplicit all the time.</p>",
        "id": 625983640,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1790072332
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This is a design problem where a universe variable gets assigned by TC, when it should be the other way around.</p>",
        "id": 625983746,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1790072361
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>And this is not even a case of implicits gone wrong. If I have to specify the type annotation with universes for each variable then lean would be very tedious to use.</p>",
        "id": 625984135,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1790072473
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You don't need universes, just writing <code>variable {V : Sort*}</code> or <code>variable {V : Type*}</code> will work fine.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I comfortably use autoImplicit all the time.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You are not a beginner.</p>",
        "id": 626033356,
        "sender_full_name": "Etienne Marion",
        "timestamp": 1790084890
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"703970\">Etienne Marion</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113488-general/topic/Weird.20defeq.20with.20and.20without.20DecidableEq.20instance/near/626033356\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>You don't need universes, just writing <code>variable {V : Sort*}</code> or <code>variable {V : Type*}</code> will work fine.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes but Type* is introducing a fresh universe variable</p>",
        "id": 626033708,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1790084955
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You said</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>If I have to specify the type annotation with universes for each variable then lean would be very tedious to use.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You don't need to.</p>",
        "id": 626038760,
        "sender_full_name": "Etienne Marion",
        "timestamp": 1790086005
    }
]